.TH tkcapture n 1.0 tkcapture "Tk Extensions"
.SH NAME
tkcapture \- Capture Tk windows, widgets and regions into photo images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBpackage require Tk 9.0\-\fR
\fBpackage require tkcapture\fR ?\fB1.0\fR?

\fBtkcapture::capture\fR \fIwindow\fR ?\fIoption value ...\fR?
\fBtkcapture::save\fR \fIwindow fileName\fR ?\fB\-format\fR \fIformat\fR? ?\fIoption value ...\fR?
\fBtkcapture::png\fR \fIwindow\fR ?\fIoption value ...\fR?
\fBtkcapture::layout\fR \fIwindow\fR
\fBtkcapture::locate\fR \fIimage x y\fR
\fBtkcapture::pkgconfig\fR \fBlist\fR|\fBget\fR ?\fIkey\fR?
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBtkcapture\fR package takes pictures of the windows of the
application that uses it.
A picture can show a whole toplevel, a single widget or any rectangle,
and is delivered as a Tk photo image, which can be displayed, compared,
written to a file or converted to data in any format that photo images
support.
.PP
Only windows of the calling application can be captured, without the
decorations that the window manager adds.
The package never reads the desktop or the windows of other
applications, so it needs no special permission on any platform.
.SH "CAPTURING"
.TP
\fBtkcapture::capture\fR \fIwindow\fR ?\fIoption value ...\fR?
.
Stores in a photo image what the toplevel of \fIwindow\fR displays in a
rectangle of \fIwindow\fR, and returns the name of the image.
By default the rectangle is the whole of \fIwindow\fR.
.RS
.PP
The image holds the pixels that the toplevel shows in the rectangle:
\fIwindow\fR itself, its descendants, and whatever else of the same
toplevel is displayed there, such as a sibling placed on top of
\fIwindow\fR.
The pixels of a widget capture therefore equal the corresponding pixels
of a capture of the whole toplevel.
\fIWindow\fR must be viewable (see \fBwinfo viewable\fR).
.PP
The following options are supported; they can be abbreviated:
.TP
\fB\-photo \fIimageName\fR
.
The existing photo image that receives the capture.
Its contents and size are replaced.
If the option is absent or empty, a new photo image is created; the
caller owns it and should delete it with \fBimage delete\fR when done.
.TP
\fB\-region\fR {\fIx1 y1 x2 y2\fR}
.
The rectangle to capture, in pixels in the coordinate system of
\fIwindow\fR.
\fIX1\fR and \fIy1\fR are the upper left corner; \fIx2\fR and \fIy2\fR
are just outside the lower right corner, as in the \fB\-from\fR option of
photo images.
The rectangle may extend beyond \fIwindow\fR, for example to show a
widget with some of its surroundings; it is clipped to the toplevel.
It is an error if nothing remains.
.TP
\fB\-scale \fImode\fR
.
\fBlogical\fR, the default, yields one image pixel per Tk pixel, so that
image coordinates are window coordinates.
\fBdevice\fR yields one image pixel per pixel of the backing store,
which gives a sharper image on a high-resolution (Retina) display on
macOS and is the same as \fBlogical\fR elsewhere.
.TP
\fB\-update \fIboolean\fR
.
If true, the default, pending redraws and geometry changes are processed
with \fBupdate idletasks\fR before the capture, so that the image shows
the current state of the widgets.
If reading the pixels makes the windowing system ask for parts of the
window to be painted that never were (on Windows, parts that were off
the screen when the window was shown), those parts are drawn the same
way and the capture is repeated.
Use false where the event loop must not be entered.
.TP
\fB\-menubar \fIboolean\fR
.
If true, and \fIwindow\fR is a toplevel with a menubar, the capturable
area extends upwards over the menubar: the default rectangle includes
it, and a \fB\-region\fR can reach it with negative y coordinates.
The option has no effect on macOS, where the menubar is not part of the
window.
The default is false.
.TP
\fB\-nocomplain \fIboolean\fR
.
If false, the default, it is an error if not a single pixel of the
rectangle could be obtained.
If true, the result is an entirely transparent image in that case.
.PP
Pixels that could not be obtained are transparent; all others are
opaque.
On X11 without a compositing window manager that concerns the parts of
a window that are off the screen or covered by another window.
.PP
A toplevel that is embedded in a container (see the \fB\-use\fR option
of \fBtoplevel\fR) displays only what lies inside the container.
The rectangle is clipped to that part.
.PP
The command merges two keys into the \fB\-metadata\fR dictionary of the
image and leaves any other key alone.
\fBDPI\fR is the resolution of the image in pixels per inch.
\fBcapture\fR is a dictionary with these keys:
.RS
.TP 18
\fBwindow\fR
\fIWindow\fR.
.TP 18
\fBtoplevel\fR
The toplevel of \fIwindow\fR.
.TP 18
\fBx\fR, \fBy\fR
The position of the upper left image pixel in the coordinates of
\fIwindow\fR.
It differs from the requested corner if the rectangle was clipped.
.TP 18
\fBrootx\fR, \fBrooty\fR
The same position in screen coordinates at the time of the capture.
.TP 18
\fBwidth\fR, \fBheight\fR
The size of the captured rectangle in Tk pixels.
.TP 18
\fBscale\fR
The number of image pixels per Tk pixel.
.TP 18
\fBcomplete\fR
False if some pixels could not be obtained and are transparent.
.TP 18
\fBwindowingsystem\fR
The result of \fBtk windowingsystem\fR.
.TP 18
\fBtime\fR
The time of the capture as returned by \fBclock milliseconds\fR.
.RE
.PP
The image pixel (\fIix\fR, \fIiy\fR) shows the point
(\fBx\fR + \fIix\fR / \fBscale\fR, \fBy\fR + \fIiy\fR / \fBscale\fR) of
\fIwindow\fR.
.RE
.SH "HELPERS"
.TP
\fBtkcapture::save\fR \fIwindow fileName\fR ?\fB\-format\fR \fIformat\fR? ?\fIoption value ...\fR?
.
Captures \fIwindow\fR with the given \fBtkcapture::capture\fR options,
writes the image to the file \fIfileName\fR and returns \fIfileName\fR.
The image file format is \fIformat\fR if given, otherwise it is derived
from the extension of \fIfileName\fR, and it is \fBpng\fR for a name
without extension.
The image is deleted again unless it was given with \fB\-photo\fR.
.TP
\fBtkcapture::png\fR \fIwindow\fR ?\fIoption value ...\fR?
.
Captures \fIwindow\fR with the given \fBtkcapture::capture\fR options
and returns the image as the bytes of a PNG file.
The image is deleted again unless it was given with \fB\-photo\fR.
.TP
\fBtkcapture::layout\fR \fIwindow\fR
.
Returns a dictionary that describes \fIwindow\fR and its viewable
descendants in the same toplevel.
Each key is a window path name; its value is a dictionary with the keys
\fBclass\fR, \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR, \fBwidth\fR and \fBheight\fR.
The coordinates are relative to \fIwindow\fR: they are the pixel
positions of the windows in a \fBlogical\fR capture of \fIwindow\fR.
Parents precede their children, and siblings are listed in stacking
order, lowest first, so where rectangles overlap the later entry is the
one displayed on top.
On X11 a menubar is displayed by a copy of its menu that Tk creates,
with a path name such as \fB.#m\fR for the menu \fB.m\fR; that copy is
listed, above the toplevel at a negative \fBy\fR.
.TP
\fBtkcapture::locate\fR \fIimage x y\fR
.
Finds the widget that the pixel (\fIx\fR, \fIy\fR) of a captured image
shows, using the \fBcapture\fR metadata of \fIimage\fR and the current
geometry of the windows.
Returns a dictionary with the keys \fBwindow\fR, the topmost window of
the captured toplevel at that point, and \fBx\fR and \fBy\fR, the point
in the coordinates of that window; they can be passed to
\fBevent generate\fR.
The result is empty if the point lies outside the toplevel.
.TP
\fBtkcapture::pkgconfig list\fR
.TP
\fBtkcapture::pkgconfig get\fR \fIkey\fR
.
Reports how the package was built, as \fBtcl::pkgconfig\fR does for Tcl.
The only key is \fBxcomposite\fR: 1 if the X11 backend reads toplevels
through the X Composite extension, 0 otherwise.
.SH "SAFE INTERPRETERS"
.PP
The package can be loaded into a safe interpreter that has Tk (see
\fBsafe::loadTk\fR), either with \fBpackage require\fR, if the package
is installed in a directory that the safe interpreter may read, or by
the trusted interpreter.
\fBtkcapture::capture\fR, \fBtkcapture::png\fR, \fBtkcapture::layout\fR
and \fBtkcapture::locate\fR work there as described;
\fBtkcapture::save\fR does not exist, because a safe interpreter may not
write files.
The trusted interpreter can fetch a picture as the result of
\fBtkcapture::png\fR.
.PP
A safe interpreter can capture its own windows only, and of those only
what lies inside the container in which its main window is embedded.
The \fB\-menubar\fR option is ignored.
If the container belongs to another application, capturing fails with
the error code \fBTK CAPTURE UNSUPPORTED\fR.
On X11 and Windows, pixels under a window of the trusted interpreter
that covers the container are transparent.
On macOS such a window is captured; an application that embeds a safe
interpreter should not display its own widgets over the container there.
.SH "ERRORS"
.PP
The commands set these error codes:
.TP
\fBTK LOOKUP WINDOW \fIname\fR
\fIName\fR is not a window.
.TP
\fBTK LOOKUP PHOTO \fIname\fR
The \fB\-photo\fR option names something that is not a photo image.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE UNMAPPED\fR
The window is not viewable.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE ICONIFIED\fR
The toplevel is iconified.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE REGION\fR
The \fB\-region\fR option is not a list of four integers.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE EMPTY_REGION\fR
Nothing remains of the region after clipping.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE OFFSCREEN\fR
No pixel of the region could be obtained, typically because it is off
the screen or covered, and \fB\-nocomplain\fR is false.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE MENUBAR\fR
The \fB\-menubar\fR option is true and the window is not a toplevel.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE UNSUPPORTED\fR
The window cannot be captured, for example because of its X visual, or
in a safe interpreter because it is embedded in another application.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE FAILED\fR
The window system did not deliver the contents of the window.
.TP
\fBTK CAPTURE NO_METADATA\fR
The image given to \fBtkcapture::locate\fR was not filled by
\fBtkcapture::capture\fR.
.PP
A capture that fails leaves no new image behind and does not change the
image given with \fB\-photo\fR.
.SH "PLATFORM NOTES"
.TP
\fBX11\fR
.
The contents are read back from the X server.
Under a compositing window manager they come from the buffer in which
it keeps the toplevel, so covered and off-screen parts are captured
correctly.
This needs the X Composite extension at build time (see
\fBtkcapture::pkgconfig\fR); without it, only covering by other
toplevels is handled.
Without a compositing window manager, as under a bare Xvfb server, the
server cannot deliver covered parts: they are transparent, as are parts
that are off the screen, and the \fBcomplete\fR entry of the metadata
is false then.
A region that is entirely off the screen is an error unless
\fB\-nocomplain\fR is true.
Where the pixels come from the window rather than the compositing
manager's buffer, a covering window makes its whole bounding rectangle
transparent, even if it is shaped or partly transparent.
Whether other toplevels can cover the window is decided by the owner
of the \fB_NET_WM_CM_S\fIn\fR selection: a compositing manager that
does not own it leads to more transparent pixels than necessary, and a
client that owns it without compositing can make pixels of toplevels
stacked over the window appear in the capture.
.TP
\fBWindows\fR
.
The desktop window manager delivers the window contents, also for
covered and off-screen parts.
Off-screen parts are drawn only when a capture asks for them; with
\fB\-update 0\fR they may come out blank.
This needs Windows 8.1 or later; on older systems the pixels are copied
from the screen and are correct only where the window is not covered.
The alpha channel of a translucent toplevel (\fBwm attributes \-alpha\fR)
is ignored: the window is captured as if it were opaque.
.TP
\fBmacOS\fR
.
The pixels are copied from the bitmap that backs the window and are
converted to the sRGB color space.
Covered and off-screen parts are captured correctly.
A window that Tk has not drawn, such as an override-redirect toplevel
placed beyond every screen, has no pixels: the capture fails with
\fBTK CAPTURE OFFSCREEN\fR unless \fB\-nocomplain\fR is true.
Tk draws all windows of a toplevel into the same bitmap, so widgets of
the application stacked over an embedded toplevel are captured with it
rather than made transparent (see \fBSAFE INTERPRETERS\fR).
.SH "USAGE NOTES"
.PP
A toplevel that has just been created is not displayed until the window
manager has mapped it, which takes more than \fBupdate idletasks\fR.
Wait for it, for example with \fBtkwait visibility\fR or \fBupdate\fR,
before capturing; otherwise the capture fails with
\fBTK CAPTURE UNMAPPED\fR.
Some window managers also map a new window off screen and move it into
place a moment later.
Without a compositing window manager, a capture taken in between fails
with \fBTK CAPTURE OFFSCREEN\fR and can be repeated after \fBupdate\fR.
.PP
A capture with \fB\-update\fR true inside a binding for
\fB<Configure>\fR or \fB<Expose>\fR can run the idle work of that same
binding again; use \fB\-update 0\fR there.
.PP
The package does not add a \fBcapture\fR subcommand to the \fBtk\fR
command.
An application that wants one can add it:
.RS
.nf
namespace ensemble configure tk \-map [dict merge \e
    [namespace ensemble configure tk \-map] \e
    {capture ::tkcapture::capture}]
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Save a whole window, one widget, and a widget with eight pixels of
context around it:
.RS
.nf
package require tkcapture

tkcapture::save . main.png
tkcapture::save .toolbar toolbar.png

set w [winfo width .f.ok]
set h [winfo height .f.ok]
tkcapture::save .f.ok ok.png \e
    \-region [list \-8 \-8 [expr {$w + 8}] [expr {$h + 8}]]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reuse one photo image for many captures:
.RS
.nf
image create photo snap
foreach page [.nb tabs] {
    .nb select $page
    tkcapture::capture $page \-photo snap
    snap write [winfo name $page].png \-format png
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Click on the widget shown at pixel (212, 40) of a capture:
.RS
.nf
set img [tkcapture::capture .]
set hit [tkcapture::locate $img 212 40]
if {[dict size $hit]} {
    dict with hit {
        event generate $window <Button\-1> \-x $x \-y $y
        event generate $window <ButtonRelease\-1> \-x $x \-y $y
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
photo(n), image(n), winfo(n), update(n), tkwait(n), safe(n)
.SH KEYWORDS
capture, image, photo, screenshot, window
